Imagine a fab increases wafer starts by 20%, using the same die design, but the fraction of good dies falls from 80% to 60%. Assume the same candidate dies per wafer and no other changes.

Before reading on, predict whether good-die output rises or falls.

Answer: it falls 10%. Relative output is 1.20 × 0.60 ÷ 0.80 = 0.90. The extra wafer starts do not compensate for the loss in yield.

These are invented teaching numbers. The point is to connect the capacity headline to the output customers can use.

5. What comes after the fab?

A working die is still not a complete AI system. It must be connected to memory, power and the rest of the computer.

Good logic dies + separately manufactured memory → packaging → servers and networking → powered, cooled clusters → software running models

This is a dependency map. Some activities happen in parallel, and their outputs must arrive together. TSMC's CoWoS technology is one example of the packaging that brings compute silicon and high-bandwidth memory together.

Now apply the same constraint logic beyond the fab.

Suppose monthly logic supply supports 100,000 accelerators, qualified memory supports 80,000, packaging supports 90,000 and site readiness allows 70,000 to enter service. Assume one product, no starting inventory and capacities already adjusted for losses.

Manufacturing is limited to 80,000 by memory. Deployment is limited to 70,000 by site readiness. Raising logic supply alone to 120,000 changes neither limit.

The lesson is not that the fab stopped mattering. Its contribution depends on what the rest of the chain can absorb.

6. Why this is the right place to move on

We have completed a first pass through fabrication, not exhausted it. Advanced lithography, materials and process integration remain part of the longer course.

But before another specialized branch, we need to understand what the fab is supplying. Packaging and memory give that knowledge a destination: a working accelerator.

Later, we will return to fabrication with sharper questions about cost, yield and the capacity needed for a particular AI system. That is how the technical detail becomes more useful on each pass.

The foundation to keep

- A chip is built through repeated, coordinated manufacturing steps.

- Yield and productive capacity determine how much usable silicon emerges.

- An essential technology is not always the step limiting additional output.

- A good die still needs memory, packaging and an operational system around it.

Before leaving Topic 1

Try explaining how a blank wafer becomes a working die in two minutes, without listing company names. Then explain why buying more EUV scanners does not automatically mean shipping more AI accelerators.

If you can connect the steps and name the assumptions, you have the foundation for the next topic. Any gaps are useful signals for review, not a reason to memorize every specialist term before proceeding.

Sources and assumptions

The fabrication recap synthesizes Lessons 001–030. All numeric examples are illustrative assumptions, not company forecasts.

